From: Bastiaan van der Plaat <bastiaan.v.d.plaat@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 07:09:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Add the DS 3D renderer

r_nds.c replaces the software renderer with one on the DS geometry
engine, which draws the world, brush and alias models, sprites and
particles on the top screen.

- Vertices are 16-bit fixed point world coordinates, surfaces are drawn
  as quad strips zig-zagging across their polygons, half as many
  polygons as triangles.
- Textures stay 8-bit in VRAM banks A, B and D, uploaded at the largest
  mip level that fits when a map loads, with the texture coordinates
  scaled to their size by the texture matrix. The world leaves 32 KiB to
  the brush models loaded after it, the ammo and health boxes, which
  share the textures with the same name. The palette goes to the
  texture palette, so palette shifts stay free. It's twice as bright,
  so the vertex light can overbright like in the software renderer.
- Light maps become vertex light per light style, so styles animate.
- T-junctions, a vertex of one face in the middle of an edge of another,
  left the hardware cracks along those edges: the vertices on the edges
  of a polygon are added to it, found through a hash of the positions.
  The hardware tells the side a polygon faces from its first three
  vertices and with the wrong one leaves the edges that run more across
  than down unfilled, so the strip of a surface starts where they aren't
  in a line for any of its quads. Surfaces are drawn until the eye is 1.5
  units behind them, the integer plane tests and the matrices round the
  eye differently, and only after both sides of their node, whose leaves
  mark them, so one seen edge on leaves no crack either.
  Dynamic lights (muzzle flashes, rockets, explosions) light the
  vertices of the surfaces R_PushDlights marked. R_LightPoint uses the
  vertex light of the nearest vertex.
- The sky has both layers, with texture coordinates following the
  direction from the eye like GLQuake, the front layer is drawn on the
  back one with the depth test for equal.
- Alias models are joined into triangle strips when a map loads, like
  GLQuake's gl_mesh.c without fans, which the hardware lacks, finding
  the next triangle of a strip through a table of the edges, and their
  byte vertices go doubled as 10-bit vertices, one write instead of two.
  Their light is cached until they move. Every texel of a skin its
  triangles don't cover, worked out at full size, takes the color of a
  covered one, as the resampled skin is sampled a little outside them,
  like the blue around the shotguns.
- The hardware data of a surface is its vertex and style counts followed
  by its texture coordinates, vertex indices and light, without pointers.
  Textures are resampled in fixed point. When a map has loaded the
  temporary memory is freed, so the cache has all the room there is.
- Alias models are placed relative to the eye, the view model in finer
  units, so their position isn't the difference of two rounded ones and
  the gun doesn't wobble while turning. The depth is w, to a 4096th of
  the distance, where z / w let monsters right behind doors show through
  them, so the near plane can be 1 unit away. Swimming, the eye is kept a
  near plane away from the surface, which would be cut out of view.
  The world's w is 3 times the gun's, like the software renderer's depth
  scale for it, so the gun doesn't poke into walls. The crosshair is drawn
  in whole pixels of the screen, 7 by 7, its edges rounded the way the
  hardware truncates them. The view is dimmed behind the menu and its questions.
  Translations are rounded to the hardware's fixed point, the projection
  is only worked out again when the field of view changes.
- The world is traversed in fixed point with frustum clip flags, it and
  the culling, surface lighting, alias models and particles run from
  ITCM as ARM code. The vertex colors of the world's surfaces are worked
  out before any of them goes to the hardware, which takes no more than
  a FIFO full of commands until it showed the last frame, so this work
  fills that wait. They are in VRAM bank H and the skins and sprite
  frames a map loads are listed in bank I, both otherwise unused, as
  the RAM is short.
- When a map loads, the file of each alias model is read once: its skins
  are uploaded from it and the model is loaded from it, of a model still
  in the cache only the part up to its triangles is read.
